<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl von Clausewitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Clausewitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl von Clausewitz was a Prussian army officer and military theorist who stressed the moral, in modern terms psychological, and political aspects of waging war. He entered Prussian service at the age of twelve, was captured at the Battle of Jena-Auerstedt in 1806, helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> reform the Prussian army, and served at Ligny and Wavre in 1815 before directing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-war-academy">Kriegsakademie</a> until 1830. He died of cholera on 16 November 1831, leaving Vom Kriege (On War) unfinished; his widow Marie published it in 1832. It is considered a seminal treatise on military strategy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-von-clausewitz.jpg" alt="Carl von Clausewitz" width="330" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Wach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clausewitz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
